# Model Change Validation — TM Alert Prioritisation Engine, Release 5.2

Independent Model Validation | Financial Crime Compliance Assurance
Ref: IMV/FCC/2026/07
Model ID: FCC-TMAP-0011
Change ref: CR-TMAP-2026-114
Issued: 2 September 2026
To: Head of Financial Crime Analytics (model owner); MLRO; Financial Crime Risk Committee

---

## 1. Why this validation was performed and at what depth

Release 5.2 changes the alert prioritisation component within the transaction monitoring
framework. It does not change the underlying rule set that generates alerts; it changes the order
in which alerts are presented to investigators and the threshold at which an alert is routed to
the enhanced review queue.

Three changes triggered validation under the Model Change Standard (MCS-2025-02 §4):

- training data extended from 18 to 30 months and re-sampled to address the 2025 typology mix
- twelve new features, principally counterparty-network and velocity derived
- revised probability cut-off for the enhanced review queue (0.41 → 0.36)

MCS-2025-02 §4.3 classifies a change as material where it alters feature composition by more than
20% or moves an operational threshold. Both apply, so this was scoped as a full re-validation
rather than a change review. The scoping determination and its basis are recorded in the
validation scoping note at Appendix A, approved by the Head of IMV on 14 July 2026.

The model supports a regulated financial-crime control. Investigators make every disposition
decision; the model affects sequence and queue routing only. This is relevant to the depth of
validation applied and is recorded here because it constrains what the model can and cannot cause.

---

## 2. Independence

IMV sits within Compliance Assurance and reports to the MLRO. No IMV staff member contributed to
the design, build, feature engineering or tuning of Release 5.2, and IMV holds no delivery
objective for the TM programme. The validator for this release, K. Prakash, previously worked in
Financial Crime Analytics until January 2024; because he worked on TMAP Release 3.x, a second
validator (A. Lim) independently re-performed the sections at §4.2 and §4.4 and the conclusion at
§8. This arrangement was agreed with the Head of IMV and is noted for the record.

---

## 3. Data and features

Training population: 30 months to 31 March 2026, 214.7m transactions across 1.34m customers,
with 9,847 alerts confirmed as productive (SAR filed or escalated to enhanced review with adverse
finding) and 186,220 alerts closed as non-productive.

IMV re-derived six of the twelve new features from source and compared against the developer's
values. Five matched to tolerance. The sixth (`ctr_network_depth_3`) differed on 3.1% of records;
the cause was traced to a difference in how self-referencing counterparty edges are excluded. The
developer's definition was found to be the intended one; the discrepancy arose from IMV's
re-derivation. No finding raised.

Label quality was examined. The productive/non-productive label depends on investigator
disposition, which is itself subject to investigator judgement and to changes in the enhanced
review procedure in October 2025. IMV re-ran the model performance measurement excluding the
three months either side of that procedure change; results at §4.1 are reported on both bases.

