## 4. Testing

### 4.1 Discrimination against the incumbent (Release 5.1)

| Measure | R5.1 | R5.2 | R5.2 excl. Oct-25 window |
|---|---|---|---|
| Alerts in top decile that were productive | 41.2% | 52.8% | 50.9% |
| Productive alerts captured in top 3 deciles | 68.4% | 79.1% | 77.6% |
| Median position of productive alert | 412 | 236 | 251 |

### 4.2 Scenario-based testing

The financial-crime analytics team constructed 64 scenarios reflecting typologies not well
represented in historical data: trade-based patterns, mule-network structures, rapid layering
through e-wallet rails, and two structuring patterns observed in industry typology sharing during
2025.

IMV re-executed all 64 independently. R5.2 prioritised the seeded activity into the top three
deciles in 51 of 64. R5.1 did so in 38 of 64. Of the 13 where R5.2 did not, 9 involved the
e-wallet rail typology; the feature set contains no e-wallet-specific velocity measure. This is
Finding IMV-02.

### 4.3 Behaviour change against the incumbent

IMV sampled 400 alerts where R5.1 and R5.2 ranked materially differently (>200 positions).

- 267: R5.2 ranked higher. Investigator review of a 60-alert subsample judged R5.2's ordering
  better justified in 44, equivalent in 11, worse in 5.
- 133: R5.1 ranked higher. Subsample of 40; R5.2 worse in 18. Of those 18, 14 were customers with
  fewer than 90 days of history, where the new network features are sparse.

Sparse-history degradation is Finding IMV-03.

### 4.4 Threshold change

Moving the enhanced review cut-off from 0.41 to 0.36 increases enhanced review volume by an
estimated 18.3% (from 2,140 to 2,532 per month on the backtest). Capacity confirmation was sought
from Financial Crime Operations; their response of 19 August 2026 confirms capacity at up to 2,600
per month with existing headcount and is at Appendix C.

IMV notes that the estimate is derived from backtest volumes and does not account for growth in
the underlying transaction base, which has run at 6–9% year on year.

