## 5. Challenge and its resolution

A challenge session was held on 12 August 2026 with the validator, the model owner, the lead
developer, the Head of Financial Crime Operations, and an observer from Internal Audit. The
validator's written challenge memo of 5 August is at Appendix B. Matters raised and outcomes:

| # | Validator challenge | Owner response | Outcome |
|---|---|---|---|
| C-1 | Label depends on investigator disposition and the procedure changed mid-window | Accepted; agreed to report on both bases | Resolved, see §4.1 |
| C-2 | e-wallet typology not covered by feature set | Accepted as a known gap; rule-based coverage exists via TM-R-118 | Accepted limitation with compensating control |
| C-3 | Sparse-history customers degraded relative to R5.1 | Partially accepted; owner argues population is small (2.7% of alerts) | Carried forward, see IMV-03 |
| C-4 | Threshold move justified on backtest volumes only | Not accepted; owner considers 2,600 headroom sufficient | Unresolved at issue date |
| C-5 | No documented minimum performance for the sparse-history segment | Accepted | Carried forward |

C-4 remains a disagreement between IMV and the model owner. IMV's position is that the capacity
margin is thin against transaction growth. The owner's position is that monthly monitoring will
surface any breach in time to act. This is recorded unresolved and was reported to the Financial
Crime Risk Committee on 28 August 2026 (minute FCRC/2026/08 item 6).

